1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,721
Want your own SonarQube? You can have one running today.

2
00:00:04,271 --> 00:00:09,285
No installer. No account. You download one zip file, unzip it, and start it.

3
00:00:09,835 --> 00:00:11,039
But there are traps.

4
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:11,942
The wrong Java.

5
00:00:11,942 --> 00:00:13,026
Too little memory.

6
00:00:13,026 --> 00:00:15,616
And a default password that everyone knows.

7
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:18,515
Today we install a real SonarQube,

8
00:00:18,515 --> 00:00:19,388
step by step,

9
00:00:19,388 --> 00:00:22,139
and then prove that it is ready and safe.

10
00:00:23,689 --> 00:00:25,118
Here is what to expect.

11
00:00:25,668 --> 00:00:27,588
One: what you need before you start.

12
00:00:28,138 --> 00:00:30,037
Two: how the versions must match.

13
00:00:30,587 --> 00:00:32,133
Three: a live install.

14
00:00:32,133 --> 00:00:33,679
Check Java and memory,

15
00:00:33,679 --> 00:00:34,101
unzip,

16
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:34,803
configure,

17
00:00:34,803 --> 00:00:35,225
start,

18
00:00:35,225 --> 00:00:35,576
wait,

19
00:00:35,576 --> 00:00:36,068
log in,

20
00:00:36,068 --> 00:00:37,755
and change the password.

21
00:00:38,305 --> 00:00:42,017
Four: how to prove it works, the common mistakes, and the lab files.

22
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:45,554
This is objective two of the SonarQube domain.

23
00:00:47,104 --> 00:00:48,661
What you need before you start.

24
00:00:49,211 --> 00:00:52,539
Java, version seventeen or newer. We have twenty one.

25
00:00:53,089 --> 00:00:57,783
Memory: a few free gigabytes. SonarQube runs three Java processes.

26
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:00,935
A free port. The default is nine thousand.

27
00:01:01,485 --> 00:01:06,584
A normal user. The search engine inside SonarQube refuses to run as root.

28
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:12,361
And a database. The built in one is for a lab only. Real teams use Postgres.

29
00:01:13,911 --> 00:01:15,532
Next, make the versions match.

30
00:01:16,082 --> 00:01:20,840
The server: ours is twenty six point eleven, the Community Build, which is free.

31
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:22,895
The scanner: for Maven,

32
00:01:22,895 --> 00:01:24,923
that is the Sonar Maven plugin.

33
00:01:24,923 --> 00:01:27,214
It sends the results to the server.

34
00:01:27,764 --> 00:01:31,668
Java and Maven must run the scanner too. Seventeen or newer.

35
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:37,551
Write down all three versions. When a scan fails, a mismatch is the first thing to check.

36
00:01:38,101 --> 00:01:41,066
And always pin the version number. Never say latest.

37
00:01:42,616 --> 00:01:43,657
Let us install.

38
00:01:43,657 --> 00:01:45,254
We start in a terminal.

39
00:01:45,254 --> 00:01:45,878
Step one,

40
00:01:45,878 --> 00:01:46,642
check Java.

41
00:01:46,642 --> 00:01:47,475
We run java,

42
00:01:47,475 --> 00:01:48,377
dash version.

43
00:01:48,377 --> 00:01:50,251
We need seventeen or newer,

44
00:01:50,251 --> 00:01:52,472
and this machine has twenty one.

45
00:01:53,022 --> 00:01:53,642
Step two,

46
00:01:53,642 --> 00:01:54,814
check the memory.

47
00:01:54,814 --> 00:01:55,641
We run free,

48
00:01:55,641 --> 00:01:56,124
dash m,

49
00:01:56,124 --> 00:01:58,191
and read the available column.

50
00:01:58,191 --> 00:02:00,052
Several gigabytes are free,

51
00:02:00,052 --> 00:02:01,086
so we are fine.

52
00:02:01,636 --> 00:02:02,360
Step three,

53
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:03,216
the download.

54
00:02:03,216 --> 00:02:07,430
In real life you get this zip from the SonarQube download page.

55
00:02:07,430 --> 00:02:09,603
Here it is already in our folder.

56
00:02:09,603 --> 00:02:11,513
We list it with L S dash L H.

57
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:13,817
It is about nine hundred megabytes.

58
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:15,096
Step four,

59
00:02:15,096 --> 00:02:15,752
unzip it.

60
00:02:15,752 --> 00:02:16,699
We run unzip,

61
00:02:16,699 --> 00:02:17,209
dash q,

62
00:02:17,209 --> 00:02:18,521
and the file name.

63
00:02:18,521 --> 00:02:20,196
It takes a few seconds.

64
00:02:20,196 --> 00:02:22,747
And that is the whole installation.

65
00:02:22,747 --> 00:02:23,694
No installer.

66
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:24,714
Just a folder.

67
00:02:29,264 --> 00:02:30,763
Look inside the folder.

68
00:02:30,763 --> 00:02:32,458
Bin has the start scripts.

69
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:33,892
Conf has the settings.

70
00:02:33,892 --> 00:02:36,760
Logs is where you look when something fails.

71
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:38,651
Extensions holds the plugins.

72
00:02:39,201 --> 00:02:39,867
Step five,

73
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:40,732
the settings.

74
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:42,198
The main file is conf,

75
00:02:42,198 --> 00:02:43,996
slash sonar.properties.

76
00:02:43,996 --> 00:02:45,860
Everything is commented out,

77
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:47,326
so the defaults apply.

78
00:02:47,326 --> 00:02:49,590
Search for the web port with grep.

79
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,805
On a small lab machine we add four lines.

80
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:53,195
First,

81
00:02:53,195 --> 00:02:55,729
bind the web server to local host only,

82
00:02:55,729 --> 00:02:58,524
so nobody else on the network can reach it.

83
00:02:59,074 --> 00:02:59,533
Second,

84
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:03,271
this line turns off memory mapping for the search engine.

85
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:05,173
Use it only on a lab machine,

86
00:03:05,173 --> 00:03:07,140
where the system limit is low.

87
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:08,255
On a real server,

88
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:10,615
raise the limit with sysctl instead.

89
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:12,222
Third and fourth,

90
00:03:12,222 --> 00:03:13,528
where the data lives.

91
00:03:13,528 --> 00:03:16,263
SonarQube checks the free disk space first,

92
00:03:16,263 --> 00:03:18,936
and this lab machine's disk is nearly full.

93
00:03:18,936 --> 00:03:22,169
So we point the data and temp folders to a RAM disk.

94
00:03:22,169 --> 00:03:23,412
On your own machine,

95
00:03:23,412 --> 00:03:24,469
keep the default,

96
00:03:24,469 --> 00:03:27,080
and make sure the disk has plenty of room.

97
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:30,894
Check the end of the file with tail. All four lines are there.

98
00:03:31,444 --> 00:03:32,105
Step six,

99
00:03:32,105 --> 00:03:32,766
start it.

100
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:34,309
The script is in bin,

101
00:03:34,309 --> 00:03:35,264
linux x86 64,

102
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:37,541
and it is called sonar.sh.

103
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:39,744
We run it with the word start.

104
00:03:39,744 --> 00:03:43,711
It starts in the background and gives the prompt back.

105
00:03:44,261 --> 00:03:45,024
Now we wait.

106
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:48,393
SonarQube starts three processes: the search engine,

107
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:49,664
then the web server,

108
00:03:49,664 --> 00:03:51,190
then the compute engine.

109
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:52,906
This takes a minute or two.

110
00:03:52,906 --> 00:03:55,703
We ask the status address every few seconds,

111
00:03:55,703 --> 00:03:56,911
and watch the word.

112
00:03:57,461 --> 00:03:58,705
The word is starting.

113
00:03:58,705 --> 00:04:02,259
That means the search engine is still preparing its indexes.

114
00:04:02,259 --> 00:04:02,910
Be patient.

115
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:04,332
If you log in too early,

116
00:04:04,332 --> 00:04:05,220
you get errors.

117
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:06,109
Do not test it,

118
00:04:06,109 --> 00:04:07,116
until it says UP.

119
00:04:07,116 --> 00:04:07,945
While we wait,

120
00:04:07,945 --> 00:04:09,426
remember the logs folder.

121
00:04:09,426 --> 00:04:11,499
SonarQube writes everything there,

122
00:04:11,499 --> 00:04:13,572
and sonar.log is the main file.

123
00:04:13,572 --> 00:04:14,697
When a start fails,

124
00:04:14,697 --> 00:04:17,244
the first error in that file tells you why.

125
00:04:17,244 --> 00:04:18,961
Our earlier try failed there,

126
00:04:18,961 --> 00:04:20,501
with a disk space message.

127
00:04:43,051 --> 00:04:43,272
UP.

128
00:04:43,272 --> 00:04:44,744
The server is ready.

129
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:45,921
Now the browser.

130
00:04:45,921 --> 00:04:47,319
We open local host,

131
00:04:47,319 --> 00:04:48,716
port nine thousand.

132
00:04:48,716 --> 00:04:50,409
This is the login page.

133
00:04:50,409 --> 00:04:52,248
The first login is admin,

134
00:04:52,248 --> 00:04:54,529
and the password is also admin.

135
00:04:55,079 --> 00:04:57,479
SonarQube makes you change it at once.

136
00:04:57,479 --> 00:04:58,834
Type the old password,

137
00:04:58,834 --> 00:04:59,757
then a new one,

138
00:04:59,757 --> 00:05:00,126
twice.

139
00:05:00,126 --> 00:05:01,050
Use a long one,

140
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:03,143
and keep it in a password manager.

141
00:05:03,693 --> 00:05:04,374
We are in.

142
00:05:04,374 --> 00:05:06,144
This is the Projects page.

143
00:05:06,144 --> 00:05:06,961
It is empty,

144
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:09,480
because nothing has been scanned yet.

145
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:11,251
The top menu has Projects,

146
00:05:11,251 --> 00:05:11,727
Issues,

147
00:05:11,727 --> 00:05:13,634
Policies and Administration.

148
00:05:14,184 --> 00:05:15,566
Open Administration,

149
00:05:15,566 --> 00:05:16,396
then System.

150
00:05:16,396 --> 00:05:19,023
At the top you see the server version.

151
00:05:19,023 --> 00:05:19,852
Scroll down,

152
00:05:19,852 --> 00:05:21,649
and it says: status is up.

153
00:05:21,649 --> 00:05:24,276
Below it are the three processes: web,

154
00:05:24,276 --> 00:05:25,313
compute engine,

155
00:05:25,313 --> 00:05:26,557
and search engine.

156
00:05:30,107 --> 00:05:32,027
Now prove it from the terminal.

157
00:05:32,027 --> 00:05:33,080
We run lab check.

158
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:34,814
It checks four things: Java,

159
00:05:34,814 --> 00:05:35,495
the server,

160
00:05:35,495 --> 00:05:36,176
the health,

161
00:05:36,176 --> 00:05:39,025
and that the default password no longer works.

162
00:05:39,575 --> 00:05:40,399
Four of four.

163
00:05:40,399 --> 00:05:42,428
The results are saved in a file.

164
00:05:42,428 --> 00:05:43,823
That is your evidence.

165
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:45,028
In the next lesson,

166
00:05:45,028 --> 00:05:46,487
we scan a real project.

167
00:05:48,037 --> 00:05:49,189
Five mistakes.

168
00:05:49,739 --> 00:05:54,304
One: starting as root. The search engine refuses. Use a normal user.

169
00:05:54,854 --> 00:05:58,780
Two: too little memory, or a port that is already used. Read the log.

170
00:05:59,330 --> 00:06:02,764
Three: keeping admin, admin. Change it on the first login.

171
00:06:03,314 --> 00:06:05,661
Four: logging in before the status says UP.

172
00:06:06,211 --> 00:06:10,648
Five: using the built in database for real work. It is for a lab only.

173
00:06:12,198 --> 00:06:14,993
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

174
00:06:15,543 --> 00:06:17,007
One: the repository,

175
00:06:17,007 --> 00:06:21,327
github.com/it-waves/devops-ai,

176
00:06:21,327 --> 00:06:23,671
in the folder academy slash lab.

177
00:06:24,221 --> 00:06:30,450
Two: the files, lab165.sh, and shortcuts165.sh.

178
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,461
Three: install it, with sudo install, into /usr/local/sbin.

179
00:06:37,011 --> 00:06:38,849
Four: load the short names,

180
00:06:38,849 --> 00:06:41,911
with source shortcuts165.sh.

181
00:06:41,911 --> 00:06:42,932
Then lab check,

182
00:06:42,932 --> 00:06:43,817
and lab stop.

183
00:06:44,367 --> 00:06:46,737
Five: set the variable SONAR_PASSWORD,

184
00:06:46,737 --> 00:06:48,172
to your admin password,

185
00:06:48,172 --> 00:06:50,105
so the health check can log in.

186
00:06:50,655 --> 00:06:55,050
Six: you download SonarQube yourself, from the official download page.

187
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:57,243
The link is in the description.

188
00:06:58,793 --> 00:06:59,668
Your turn.

189
00:07:00,218 --> 00:07:03,012
One: check your Java version, and your free memory.

190
00:07:03,562 --> 00:07:06,656
Two: download SonarQube, unzip it, and start it.

191
00:07:07,206 --> 00:07:10,576
Three: wait for UP, then log in, and change the password.

192
00:07:11,126 --> 00:07:13,644
Four: run lab check, and get four of four.

193
00:07:14,194 --> 00:07:16,626
Five: write down the three versions you use.

194
00:07:17,176 --> 00:07:18,904
Pause the video, and do it now.

195
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:35,329
Quick check.

196
00:07:35,879 --> 00:07:38,012
Which Java version does the scanner need?

197
00:07:38,562 --> 00:07:39,970
Why must you wait for UP?

198
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:42,589
Why change admin, admin at once?

199
00:07:43,139 --> 00:07:45,763
Why is the built in database only for a lab?

200
00:07:46,313 --> 00:07:48,599
Objective covered: MC18,

201
00:07:48,599 --> 00:07:49,599
objective two.

202
00:07:49,599 --> 00:07:52,884
Install and connect a compatible lab instance.

203
00:07:53,434 --> 00:07:55,503
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

204
00:08:06,053 --> 00:08:09,573
Next: scan a real Maven project, and see the results.

205
00:08:10,123 --> 00:08:13,451
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
